{"step_index":641,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:06:35Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:06:35Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:06:36Z\nShowing lines 1-234 of 234\n1: ---\n2: name: grpc-expert\n3: description: \"Expert guidance for gRPC/Protocol Buffers work in the operation-dbus-proto (OP-DBUS) workspace — designing plugin methods, generating .proto/service definitions, wiring the op-grpc-bridge, or fixing plugins with missing gRPC methods. Use this whenever the user mentions gRPC, proto/protobuf, plugin methods, the gRPC bridge, tonic, reflection descriptors, 'missing plugins' in the gRPC diagnostic page, or the seal/freeze/hot pipeline — even if they don't say 'gRPC' explicitly (e.g. 'the plugin isn't showing up on the diagnostic page', 'add a method to plugin X', 'regen protos').\"\n4: metadata:\n5:   version: 1.0.0\n6: disable-model-invocation: false\n7: ---\n8: \n9: # gRPC Expert — OP-DBUS\n10: \n11: This workspace does **not** hand-write per-plugin `.proto` files. Proto generation is\n12: automatic, driven entirely by each plugin's `PluginSchema`. If you remember one thing\n13: from this skill: **fixing a plugin's gRPC surface means editing its Rust schema\n14: function, not writing `.proto`.**\n15: \n16: ## The real pipeline (roll → seal → freeze → hot)\n17: \n18: ```\n19: 1. Plugin declares  → PluginSchema { methods: HashMap<String, MethodDecl>, signals: Vec<SignalDecl>, ... }\n20:                        in crates/op-plugins/src/state_plugins/<plugin>.rs\n21: 2. Schema gets put   → op-projection's SchemaEngine publishes the catalog hash\n22:                        (op_identity::schema_bridge::schema_catalog_hash())\n23: 3. Sealed by blob    → op-blob seals the plugin object into an immutable blob:\n24:                        /dev/shm/opdbus/plugin-blobs/<plugin_id>.<schema_hash16>.blob\n25:                        (op-blob is the ONLY writer — never write blobs elsewhere)\n26: 4. Descriptors       → TWO separate reflection layers exist, don't confuse them:\n27:    for reflection       a) STATIC: crates/op-grpc-bridge/build.rs generates\n28:                             plugin_methods.proto from every plugin's schema.methods,\n29:                             compiles it with tonic_build into operation_descriptor.bin\n30:                             (a protobuf FileDescriptorSet), served by tonic-reflection.\n31:                          b) DYNAMIC: crates/op-grpc-bridge/src/dynamic_reflection.rs\n32:                             (ActiveReflectionCatalog) hydrates live from the sealed\n33:                             SHM blob catalog via hydrate_reflection_from_shm() —\n34:                             this is the \"frozen\" descriptor set for what's actually\n35:                             present right now, independent of what's compiled in.\n36: 5. Frozen            → the blob + its descriptors are immutable once sealed.\n37: 6. gRPC socket = hot → crates/op-grpc-bridge/src/grpc_server.rs registers the\n38:                        generated per-plugin service (plugin_method_routes.rs,\n39:                        add_routes()) on the tonic UDS bridge, dispatching each\n40:                        RPC to call_generated_plugin_method_typed(plugin_id,\n41:                        schema_name, ...) which routes through PluginService.CallMethod\n42:                        — i.e. gRPC never bypasses D-Bus, it's a bridge, not a\n43:                        second control plane.\n44: ```\n45: \n46: A plugin shows green/connected on the gRPC diagnostic page **iff**:\n47: - its `PluginSchema.methods` is non-empty (empty → `build.rs` silently skips it —\n48:   see `collect_plugin_methods()`), **and**\n49: - the plugin is sealed in the SHM blob catalog.\n50: \n51: These are discovery prerequisites, not proof of execution. Canonical dispatch\n52: also requires the method's complete declaration and plugin version to match the\n53: compiled handler, argument validation, verified identity, and its exact grant.\n54: An advertised method whose adapter is absent is unfinished work.\n55: \n56: ## Where to actually make changes\n57: \n58: | Task | File |\n59: |---|---|\n60: | Add/fix methods for plugin `<name>` | `crates/op-plugins/src/state_plugins/<name>.rs`, inside `<name>_schema()` |\n61: | Reusable input/output builders | `crates/op-plugins/src/state_plugins/plugin_scaffold_helpers.rs` |\n62: | Regenerate the derived `.proto` + routes | Just rebuild — `build.rs` regenerates on every `cargo build -p op-grpc-bridge` (it `rerun-if-changed`s `../op-plugins/src/state_plugins`) |\n63: | Wire a genuinely new (non-plugin-derived) service | `crates/op-grpc-bridge/proto/*.proto`, add to the `compile_protos` list + `rerun-if-changed` in `crates/op-grpc-bridge/build.rs`, then register in `crates/op-grpc-bridge/src/grpc_server.rs` |\n64: | Inspect what's currently generated | `target/{debug,release}/build/op-grpc-bridge-*/out/plugin_methods.proto` (build artifact — never hand-edit) |\n65: \n66: **Never** create files under a `/proto/plugin_methods/` directory or a\n67: `plugin_methods_unified.proto` by hand — those paths don't exist in this repo and\n68: aren't part of the real pipeline; that was a stale spec from an earlier session.\n69: The single source of truth is `schema.methods`.\n70: \n71: ## Adding methods to a plugin (the actual workflow)\n72: \n73: Look at `crates/op-plugins/src/state_plugins/proxy_server.rs` (`proxy_server_schema()`)\n74: for the canonical pattern:\n75: \n76: ```rust\n77: use op_state_store::{MethodDecl, PluginSchema, SideEffect};\n78: \n79: schema.methods.insert(\n80:     \"start_proxy\".to_string(),\n81:     method_decl_from_schemars_with_output::<StartProxyInput, plugin_scaffold_helpers::AckOutput>(\n82:         \"start_proxy\",\n83:         SideEffect::Mutation,       // Read | Mutation\n84:         false,                       // idempotent?\n85:         \"cap.network.proxy.start@v1\",   // required_capability\n86:         \"mut.network.proxy.start@v1\",   // subid — must follow the taxonomy in CLAUDE.md\n87:     ),\n88: );\n89: ```\n90: \n91: - Define `StartProxyInput` (and a typed output struct, or reuse `AckOutput`) as\n92:   `#[derive(schemars::JsonSchema)]` structs — the JSON Schema is derived, never\n93:   hand-written as `serde_json::json!({...})` unless there's no existing helper.\n94: - `method_decl_from_schemars_with_output::<Input, Output>` is the current\n95:   (non-deprecated) helper; the single-generic `method_decl_from_schemars` is\n96:   deprecated but still present for legacy callers.\n97: - `subid` must be registered in the canonical registry per CLAUDE.md\n98:   (`crates/op-plugins/src/state_plugins/oscal_subid_registry.rs`) — CI enforces\n99:   uniqueness.\n100: - For plugins with **zero** declared methods today (the ones missing from the\n101:   diagnostic page), don't hand-author their methods one by one — see\n102:   **The Auto-Creator Plugin** below. That's the actual deliverable: a plugin\n103:   that finds these gaps and fills them itself, using real research per plugin.\n104: \n105: ## The Auto-Creator Plugin (an ongoing capability, not a one-off batch)\n106: \n107: This is **not** a one-time task to fix ~11 named plugins. It's a standing\n108: system capability: whenever the system encounters *any* missing plugin —\n109: now, or a new one added in the future — it automatically generates that\n110: plugin and submits it for **human review**, never auto-merges/auto-seals it\n111: unreviewed. The current ~11 gaps are just the first real instances that\n112: exercise this, not the scope.\n113: \n114: Consistent with this workspace's \"reactive, not polled\" principle (CLAUDE.md):\n115: this should be triggered by an actual arrival/lookup that reveals the gap\n116: (e.g. a D-Bus/gRPC call or reflection lookup against a plugin whose\n117: `schema.methods` is empty, or a plugin name that doesn't resolve at all),\n118: not a cron-style sweep re-scanning all plugins on a timer.\n119: \n120: Build it as a plugin itself (same `PluginSchema`/`MethodDecl` pattern as every\n121: plugin in `crates/op-plugins/src/state_plugins/`), whose job is to:\n122: \n123: 1. **Detect a gap on encounter** — when something references a plugin that's\n124:    missing or has `schema.methods.is_empty()` (same emptiness check\n125:    `build.rs::collect_plugin_methods` already uses to skip plugins), that's\n126:    the trigger — not a periodic scan of the whole registry.\n127: 2. **Research that specific gap** — drive deep research via the **NotebookLM\n128:    MCP** (once wired up) to determine what capabilities/operations the\n129:    missing plugin should realistically expose, given its existing state\n130:    struct (if any), documentation, and prior art from comparable plugins\n131:    already in the registry. This replaces guessing or generic Get/List/Watch\n132:    stubs with a grounded, plugin-specific proposal.\n133: 3. **Synthesize typed schemas** — turn the research output into real Rust\n134:    input/output structs (`#[derive(schemars::JsonSchema)]`) and `MethodDecl`\n135:    entries — per the \"no `AckOutput`\" rule above, every synthesized method\n136:    needs its own meaningful typed request/response, not a generic ack.\n137: 4. **Submit for human review — do not auto-apply.** The output is a proposed\n138:    plugin/schema (e.g. a diff/PR against `<name>_schema()`, or a new\n139:    `crates/op-plugins/src/state_plugins/<name>.rs` for a wholly new plugin),\n140:    held for a human to approve before it's merged. Only after human approval\n141:    does it enter the normal pipeline (build → proto → reflection → sealed\n142:    blob → hot). The auto-creator must never seal a blob or register a plugin\n143:    into the live catalog on its own. It never emits `.proto` directly and\n144:    never writes to `/proto/plugin_methods/` — the single source of truth\n145:    stays `schema.methods`, authored by a human-approved change.\n146: \n147: In short: this is a **standing, reactive meta-plugin that drafts schemas for\n148: whatever plugin is found missing, research-backed via NotebookLM, gated by\n149: human review** — not a fixed batch job for a specific list of plugins.\n150: \n151: ## JSON Schema → proto mapping\n152: \n153: The shared mapper lives in `crates/op-blob/src/json_schema_proto.rs`; the bridge\n154: uses it for generated native messages. Structured objects become typed nested\n155: messages; typed string-key maps retain their value types. Scalars preserve\n156: presence, including explicit false, zero and empty strings. Unsigned 64-bit\n157: contracts use `uint64`. Nulls, arbitrary JSON, unions and nested arrays use the\n158: appropriate standard `Value`/`Struct`/`ListValue` dependencies. Arbitrary numbers\n159: inside `Value` remain subject to protobuf's double-precision limit.\n160: \n161: Two existing wire layouts have distinct numbering rules: generated native\n162: bridge fields use stable FNV-1a numbers; sealed per-method messages retain their\n163: sorted sequential numbering. Preserve both layouts. Canonical schema map keys\n164: and declared transport method names are separate identities, not interchangeable\n165: aliases. Consumers register the original sealed descriptors and never reseal,\n166: regenerate, or hash them as an existence check.\n167: \n168: ## Known project-specific tensions vs. generic gRPC best practice\n169: \n170: Be aware of these before applying textbook advice unmodified:\n171: \n172: - **Do not use `AckOutput { success: bool }` for new/generated methods.**\n173:   It exists in the codebase (see `proxy_server.rs`) as a legacy shortcut for\n174:   \"no meaningful return value,\" but every method a plugin exposes should have\n175:   a real, dedicated typed **input** struct and typed **output** struct that\n176:   reflect what the operation actually consumes and returns — not a generic\n177:   bool. This matters especially for the auto-creator (below): when it\n178:   synthesizes methods for a previously-empty plugin, it must generate a\n179:   specific `<Method>Input` and `<Method>Output` schemars struct per method,\n180:   never fall back to `AckOutput`. Errors still go through `tonic::Status` /\n181:   D-Bus error replies, never a `success: false` field.\n182: - **Enums are flattened to `string`, not real proto `enum`.** JSON Schema\n183:   `enum` currently maps to proto `string` (see table above), so the classic\n184:   `FOO_STATUS_UNSPECIFIED = 0` convention isn't applied by the generator today.\n185:   If asked to add real proto enums, that requires changing\n186:   `json_schema_type_to_proto` in `crates/op-grpc-bridge/build.rs`, not just the\n187:   plugin schema.\n188: - **Streaming comes from state in SHM.** The owner reaffirmed this on\n189:   2026-09-13. Methods update authoritative SHM state before notifications;\n190:   existing state subscriptions read that state. Current generated method RPCs\n191:   being unary does not require another streaming method/service. Preserve\n192:   private principal/session scope. Method arguments, event payloads and\n193:   generator buffers are not independent state authorities, and buffered unary\n194:   responses are not a substitute for SHM state streaming.\n195: - **One generated file per bridge crate**, not the \"1-1-1\" file-per-message\n196:   convention — `plugin_methods.proto` bundles every plugin's messages/services\n197:   in one generated file. That's fine; it's a build artifact, not hand-maintained\n198:   source, so the usual \"small modular .proto files\" guidance doesn't apply to it.\n199: \n200: ## Generic protobuf/gRPC conventions (still apply to hand-written `.proto`)\n201: \n202: For any `.proto` you *do* hand-write (domain services under\n203: `crates/op-grpc-bridge/proto/`, not plugin-derived ones):\n204: \n205: - `snake_case` field names, `CamelCase` message/service/RPC names, package names\n206:   lowercase (this repo uses `operation.plugin.v1`-style packages).\n207: - Reserve field numbers for removed fields; never reuse them.\n208: - Prefer `optional` for fields that may be absent; avoid letting one field's\n209:   value change the semantic meaning of another.\n210: - Real proto `enum`s (where you control the generator, e.g. hand-written\n211:   services) always start with `..._UNSPECIFIED = 0`.\n212: - Use gRPC status codes (`tonic::Status`) for error paths; reserve response\n213:   message fields for actual data.\n214: - TLS is mandatory in this workspace (per CLAUDE.md zero-trust transport) —\n215:   never wire a new gRPC service as plaintext, even over UDS.\n216: - Server reflection (`tonic-reflection`) must stay wired for every new service\n217:   so it's discoverable by `op-cognitive-mcp` and the gRPC diagnostic page —\n218:   don't add a service that bypasses reflection registration.\n219: \n220: ## Quick diagnostic checklist\n221: \n222: If a plugin isn't showing up / isn't callable over gRPC:\n223: \n224: 1. Does `crates/op-plugins/src/state_plugins/<name>.rs`'s `<name>_schema()`\n225:    actually populate `schema.methods`? (Empty → `build.rs` skips it entirely.)\n226: 2. Is the plugin registered via `inventory::submit! { PluginReg::new(...) }` in\n227:    its own file (self-registration — check `crates/op-plugins/src/default_registry.rs`\n228:    only if the plugin doesn't show up in `available_plugins()` at all)?\n229: 3. Did `cargo build -p op-grpc-bridge` actually rerun? (`build.rs` only reruns on\n230:    changes under `../op-plugins/src/state_plugins` or `build.rs` itself.)\n231: 4. Is the plugin actually **sealed** in the SHM blob catalog\n232:    (`/dev/shm/opdbus/plugin-blobs/`)? Static compiled-in proto support and the\n233:    *dynamic* \"hot\" reflection/routing are two different gates — a plugin can\n234:    compile fine and still be invisible if it was never sealed."}
{"step_index":642,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:06:35Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:06:35Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:06:36Z\nShowing lines 1-63 of 63\n1: ---\n2: name: grpc-protocol-expert\n3: description: >-\n4:   Generic specialist in gRPC protocol, streaming RPCs, Protocol Buffers, channel\n5:   config, load balancing, auth, and observability. Use for general gRPC/protobuf\n6:   work outside the OP-DBUS PluginSchema seal/freeze/hot pipeline. For OP-DBUS\n7:   plugin gRPC surfaces, prefer the grpc-expert skill instead.\n8: disable-model-invocation: false\n9: ---\n10: \n11: # gRPC Protocol Expert\n12: \n13: ## Focus Areas\n14: \n15: - gRPC protocol intricacies and best practices\n16: - Unary, server-streaming, client-streaming, and bidirectional streaming RPCs\n17: - Protocol Buffers (protobuf) for efficient serialization\n18: - Service definition and implementation in gRPC\n19: - Channel configuration and management\n20: - Load balancing strategies within gRPC\n21: - gRPC authentication and authorization mechanisms\n22: - Network optimization for gRPC communication\n23: - Observability setups, including logging, tracing, and metrics\n24: - Efficient handling of gRPC errors and status codes\n25: \n26: ## Approach\n27: \n28: - Begin with a clear understanding of service requirements before implementing\n29: - Use Protocol Buffers for defining service interfaces and messages\n30: - Implement efficient error handling with gRPC status codes\n31: - Leverage streaming for real-time data processing where applicable\n32: - Optimize network usage by compressing messages and headers\n33: - Employ deadline and timeouts for better control over communication\n34: - Choose appropriate load balancing strategies for scalability\n35: - Configure multiple channels and target services for robustness\n36: - Utilize SSL/TLS for secure communication\n37: - Implement structured logging, tracing, and metrics setup for observability\n38: \n39: ## Quality Checklist\n40: \n41: - Thoroughly defined .proto files adhering to defined conventions\n42: - Service implementation matches the .proto specification\n43: - Correctly configured server and client channels\n44: - Stream types appropriately used based on data flow needs\n45: - Efficient serialization and deserialization processes\n46: - Comprehensive unit and integration testing for gRPC calls\n47: - Implemented error handling with descriptive status codes\n48: - Adequate logging of gRPC requests and responses\n49: - Metrics capturing for latency, error rates, and payload size\n50: - Secure communication ensured with proper encryption standards\n51: \n52: ## Output\n53: \n54: - Clear and comprehensive .proto files defining all services and methods\n55: - High-performance gRPC services with optimized channel settings\n56: - Robust client applications with efficient service consumers\n57: - Detailed logging and monitoring setup for gRPC calls\n58: - Secure and scalable gRPC-based systems\n59: - Reliable streaming implementations for real-time data\n60: - Documentation including gRPC integration guides and best practices\n61: - Load testing results showing stable performance under expected traffic\n62: - Error handling guides for service developers\n63: - Benchmarks demonstrating gRPC performance improvements over alternatives"}
{"step_index":643,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:06:35Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:06:35Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:06:36Z\nShowing lines 1-377 of 377\n1: ---\n2: name: json-render\n3: description: >-\n4:   Expert skill for building json-render.dev catalog components, page specs, and\n5:   generative UI across three repos: /srv/git/json-render (library source),\n6:   /srv/git/odbus (Rust backend + op-web Axum server), and\n7:   /srv/git/3tched.com (separate chatbot-rendered UI repository).\n8:   Covers the full stack: defineCatalog → Zod schema → React component →\n9:   pageSpec → NAV_MANIFEST → factory plugin wiring → sealed blob pipeline →\n10:   gallery/catalog promotion gate. Also covers the D-Bus plugin schema for\n11:   json-render and schema-renderer in op-plugins.\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # json-render Skill\n15: \n16: ## Local deployment policy — runit, not systemd\n17: \n18: The user-designated UI repository is `/srv/git/3tched.com`, separate\n19: from `/srv/git/odbus`. Inspect its actual catalog, registry and build/sync inputs;\n20: the reference layouts below do not establish its implemented components.\n21: `/srv/git/operation-dashboard-ui-07` and `crates/op-web/ui/` are retired UI paths.\n22: \n23: The host uses runit. Backend runtime actions go through the authenticated D-Bus\n24: plugin surface. Only for authorized operator service work use `sudo -n sv`\n25: (for example `sudo -n sv status op-web`); never use `systemctl`, its shim, s6 or\n26: service6. Edit versioned runit inputs, not `/run/runit/service`. Backend deployment\n27: is btrfs send/receive of an explicitly prepared filesystem snapshot; there is no\n28: deployment script. Never hand-copy binaries. Static UI synchronization does not itself\n29: require an op-web restart. Do not create a parallel frontend backend or expose\n30: broker credentials to the browser.\n31: \n32: EMQX is the retained vendor-binary-backed `emqx` D-Bus plugin, independent of\n33: retired Netmaker. Broker events may drive authorized live state updates while\n34: the chatbot generates schema-pinned layout patches. A generated mode, component\n35: or action never grants permissions: enforce verified session and project ACLs\n36: server-side, including on streamed updates and reconnects.\n37: \n38: ## Overview — Three Repos, One System\n39: \n40: ```\n41: /srv/git/json-render                   ← upstream library (source of truth for API)\n42: /srv/git/odbus                         ← Rust backend\n43:   crates/op-plugins/src/state_plugins/json_render.rs    D-Bus plugin schema\n44:   crates/op-plugins/src/state_plugins/schema_renderer.rs\n45:   crates/op-web/src/handlers/ui_model.rs                Axum gallery/catalog routes\n46: /srv/git/3tched.com        ← active UI target; paths below are layout examples\n47:   src/json-render/\n48:     catalog/catalog.ts                 defineCatalog (app catalog contract)\n49:     catalog/registry.tsx               defineRegistry (component implementations)\n50:     catalog/components/                React component render functions\n51:       shell.tsx   dashboard.tsx   network.tsx   primitives.tsx\n52:     navigation/manifest.ts             NAV_MANIFEST (sidebar)\n53:     navigation/capabilities.ts\n54:     navigation/icons.ts\n55:     pages/                             pageSpec files per route\n56:     runtime/                           JsonRenderProvider, SpecPage, PageSpecOutlet\n57:     shell/shellSpec.ts                 shell composition spec\n58:     spec-builders.ts                   dataToSpec / streamToSpec helpers\n59: ```\n60: \n61: ---\n62: \n63: ## 1. Spec Wire Format\n64: \n65: ```typescript\n66: interface Spec {\n67:   root: string;                         // key of the root element\n68:   elements: Record<string, UIElement>;  // FLAT map — no nesting\n69: }\n70: \n71: interface UIElement {\n72:   type: string;                         // catalog component name\n73:   props: Record<string, unknown>;       // validated by catalog Zod schema\n74:   children?: string[];                  // keys into elements map\n75:   slots?: Record<string, string[]>;     // named slot overrides\n76:   visible?: VisibilityCondition;        // { $state: \"path\" } or literal bool\n77:   on?: Record<string, ActionBinding>;   // event → action\n78:   repeat?: { statePath: string; key?: string };\n79:   watch?: Record<string, ActionBinding>;\n80: }\n81: ```\n82: \n83: **Critical:** `elements` is a flat key-value map. Children are string keys, not\n84: nested objects. `dataToSpec`/`streamToSpec` in `spec-builders.ts` handle this.\n85: \n86: Dynamic prop state references:\n87: ```typescript\n88: { collapsed: { $state: \"/shell/navCollapsed\" } }  // resolved at render time\n89: { collapsed: false }                               // literal value\n90: ```\n91: \n92: ---\n93: \n94: ## 2. Catalog Contract (reference layout)\n95: \n96: **File:** `src/json-render/catalog/catalog.ts`\n97: \n98: ```typescript\n99: import { defineCatalog } from \"@json-render/core\";\n100: import { schema } from \"@json-render/react/schema\";\n101: import { z } from \"zod\";\n102: \n103: export const appCatalog = defineCatalog(schema, {\n104:   components: {\n105:     myComponent: {\n106:       props: z.object({\n107:         title: z.string(),\n108:         subtitle: z.string().nullable(),  // nullable = omittable in specs\n109:         variant: z.enum([\"default\",\"ok\",\"warn\",\"danger\"]).nullable(),\n110:       }),\n111:       slots: [\"default\"],\n112:       description: \"One-line LLM prompt description.\",\n113:     },\n114:   },\n115:   actions: {\n116:     navigate: {\n117:       params: z.object({ route: z.string() }),\n118:       description: \"Navigate the router to a route path.\",\n119:     },\n120:   },\n121: });\n122: ```\n123: \n124: **Rules:**\n125: - Every component needs a `description` — feeds `catalog.prompt()` for generative runs.\n126: - Use `z.nullable()` not `z.optional()` — spec JSON uses `null`, not missing keys.\n127: - Never invent props outside the Zod schema — specs are validated before render.\n128: - camelCase names in TypeScript catalog; lowercase-hyphen in egui DSL.\n129: \n130: ---\n131: \n132: ## 3. React Component Implementation\n133: \n134: **Pattern:** Use `El<\"componentName\">` from `catalog/components/types.ts`.\n135: \n136: ```typescript\n137: // src/json-render/catalog/components/myFeature.tsx\n138: import type { El } from \"./types\";\n139: \n140: export const MyComponentEl: El<\"myComponent\"> = ({ props, children, emit }) => (\n141:   <div>\n142:     <h2>{props.title}</h2>\n143:     {props.subtitle && <p>{props.subtitle}</p>}\n144:     <button onClick={() => emit(\"press\")}>Go</button>\n145:     {children}\n146:   </div>\n147: );\n148: ```\n149: \n150: Register in `src/json-render/catalog/registry.tsx`:\n151: ```typescript\n152: myComponent: myFeature.MyComponentEl,\n153: ```\n154: \n155: Component anatomy:\n156: - `props` — typed, already validated against Zod schema\n157: - `children` — React nodes from `slots[\"default\"]` or `children[]`\n158: - `emit(eventName)` — fires action bound to `on.eventName` in the spec\n159: - Read live data internally (hooks/stores) — specs stay pure composition\n160: \n161: ---\n162: \n163: ## 4. Adding a Page (reference layout)\n164: \n165: ### Step 1 — NAV_MANIFEST entry\n166: **File:** `src/json-render/navigation/manifest.ts`\n167: \n168: ```typescript\n169: {\n170:   id: \"factory\",\n171:   label: \"Factory\",\n172:   route: \"/factory\",\n173:   icon: \"Factory\",        // any Lucide icon name\n174:   section: \"Agent\",       // must be in SECTION_ORDER\n175:   order: 45,\n176:   wip: true,\n177:   wipReason: \"Sessions tab not yet wired to factory.list_sessions.\",\n178: }\n179: ```\n180: \n181: Sections (SECTION_ORDER): `\"Chat\"`, `\"UI Model\"`, `\"Control\"`, `\"Agent\"`,\n182: `\"Infrastructure\"`, `\"ZeroClaw\"`, `\"Settings\"`.\n183: \n184: ### Step 2 — Page spec\n185: **File:** `src/json-render/pages/factory.pagespec.ts`\n186: \n187: ```typescript\n188: import type { Spec } from \"@json-render/core\";\n189: \n190: export const factorySpec: Spec = {\n191:   root: \"root\",\n192:   elements: {\n193:     root: {\n194:       type: \"container\",\n195:       props: {},\n196:       children: [\"header\", \"row1\"],\n197:     },\n198:     header: {\n199:       type: \"pageHeader\",\n200:       props: {\n201:         title: \"Factory\",\n202:         subtitle: \"Session management · model routing · computer inventory\",\n203:       },\n204:     },\n205:     row1: {\n206:       type: \"grid\",\n207:       props: { cols: 2, gap: 4, className: \"grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2\" },\n208:       children: [\"sessions\", \"computers\"],\n209:     },\n210:     sessions: { type: \"factorySessionsPanel\", props: {} },\n211:     computers: { type: \"factoryComputersPanel\", props: {} },\n212:   },\n213: };\n214: ```\n215: \n216: ### Step 3 — Register in pages/index.ts\n217: ```typescript\n218: export const PAGE_SPECS: Record<string, Spec> = {\n219:   \"/\": overviewSpec,\n220:   \"/network\": networkSpec,\n221:   \"/factory\": factorySpec,\n222: };\n223: ```\n224: \n225: If still using a legacy React page, skip step 3 and register in `App.tsx` router instead.\n226: \n227: ---\n228: \n229: ## 5. Factory Plugin — gRPC Methods\n230: \n231: Generated files in `src/grpc/gen/plugin_methods/`:\n232: \n233: | Subid | Input | Output |\n234: |---|---|---|\n235: | `factory.list_sessions` | `{}` | `sessions: Value[]` |\n236: | `factory.get_session` | `{}` | `session?: Value` |\n237: | `factory.list_models` | `{}` | `models: Value[]` |\n238: | `factory.list_computers` | `{}` | `computers: Value[]` |\n239: | `factory.discover_byom` | `{}` | `{}` |\n240: | `factory.set_autonomy` | `{}` | `{}` |\n241: \n242: Call pattern (see AntigravityService in `src/grpc/client.ts`):\n243: ```typescript\n244: import { getPluginMethodsClient } from \"@/grpc/client\";\n245: \n246: const client = getPluginMethodsClient();\n247: const { response } = await client.callSubid({\n248:   subid: \"factory.list_sessions\",\n249:   input: {},\n250: });\n251: // response.output is a protobuf Value — unwrap with valueToJson()\n252: ```\n253: \n254: ---\n255: \n256: ## 6. Factory / odbus Architecture Rules\n257: \n258: From `FACTORY-HANDOFF.md` and `CLAUDE.md` in `/srv/git/odbus`:\n259: \n260: - Plugin IS schema. Schema lives in the plugin `.rs` file, not in `plugin_schema_defs.rs`.\n261: - D-Bus object existence = system existence. Plugin objects at `/org/opdbus/v1/plugins/<name>`.\n262: - `uuid` = machine identity. Never replace with `subid`.\n263: - `subid` = OSCAL operational taxonomy key. Must be an OSCAL prop value, not remarks.\n264: - `mut.*` records require `actor_id` and `capability_id`.\n265: - `evt.*` records require `event_id` or `event_hash`.\n266: - No `bool success` fields — use gRPC status codes.\n267: - No JSON string payloads — use `google.protobuf.Struct`.\n268: - Compliance mappings belong in metadata arrays, not inside the `subid` string.\n269: \n270: tched_router context:\n271: \n272: - `tched_router` is the OP-DBUS scaffolding for the autonomous `zeroclaw` binary.\n273: - `op-grpc-bridge`, not that binary, owns the `:8090` TLS fabric. Cognitive MCP\n274:   is in-process; do not start a standalone cognitive MCP runit service.\n275: - The factory examples above are historical; verify current methods, input\n276:   types, capabilities and bus names from the sealed plugin surface before use.\n277: \n278: ---\n279: \n280: ## 7. Gallery → Catalog Promotion Pipeline\n281: \n282: ```\n283: tched_router reads plugin PluginSchema blob\n284:   ↓ (sealed blob at /dev/shm/opdbus/plugin-blobs/<plugin>.<hash>.blob)\n285: gemma_brain / op-gallery-gen generates Spec\n286:   ↓\n287: /api/gallery-gen/* (op-web Axum: ui_model.rs) → /dev/shm/ui-specs.json\n288:   ↓\n289: Gallery page (/gallery) — human reviews\n290:   ↓ human promotes\n291: Catalog page (/catalog) — approved specs only\n292:   ↓\n293: <SpecPage> renders in React dashboard\n294: ```\n295: \n296: Schema hash pinning (critical — see `.specs/generative-ui-catalog/vision.md`):\n297: - Gallery entries MUST carry the `schema_hash` they were drawn against.\n298: - Catalog entries are PINNED to that hash.\n299: - A reseal identifies which approved elements are stale.\n300: - Never promote without verifying hash matches the current sealed blob.\n301: - Plugin presence comes from the current sealed manifest; counts change with reseals.\n302: \n303: ---\n304: \n305: ## 8. D-Bus json_render Plugin\n306: \n307: **File:** `crates/op-plugins/src/state_plugins/json_render.rs`\n308: \n309: Publishes the full json-render surface at `/org/opdbus/v1/plugins/json_render`:\n310: - `packages` — `@json-render/*` package inventory\n311: - `components` — catalog component declarations\n312: - `actions` — action type declarations\n313: - `validation_checks` — built-in validation rules\n314: - `directives` — `$format`, `$math`, `$concat`, `$count`, etc.\n315: - `methods` — D-Bus method surface\n316: - `inspector_fields` — uncapped fields from authoritative packages\n317: - `source` — provenance (docs URL, repo URL, commit hash)\n318: \n319: This blob is what generative runs read to know the available surface area.\n320: \n321: ---\n322: \n323: ## 9. spec-builders Utility\n324: \n325: **File:** `src/json-render/spec-builders.ts`\n326: \n327: ```typescript\n328: import { dataToSpec, streamToSpec } from \"@/json-render\";\n329: \n330: // Any JSON → renderable spec (uses card/kv/container)\n331: const spec = dataToSpec(myJsonObject, \"My Label\");\n332: \n333: // List of streaming messages → spec\n334: const spec = streamToSpec(messageArray, \"Stream Title\");\n335: ```\n336: \n337: Use when rendering raw gRPC response data without a custom component.\n338: \n339: ---\n340: \n341: ## 10. Checklist: Adding a New Feature Page\n342: \n343: - [ ] Add `NavItem` to `NAV_MANIFEST` in `navigation/manifest.ts`\n344: - [ ] Create `src/json-render/pages/<name>.pagespec.ts` with a typed `Spec`\n345: - [ ] Register in `pages/index.ts` PAGE_SPECS map (or use legacy React route)\n346: - [ ] If new components needed:\n347:   - [ ] Add to `catalog/catalog.ts` (Zod schema + description)\n348:   - [ ] Implement in `catalog/components/<file>.tsx` using `El<\"name\">` type\n349:   - [ ] Register in `catalog/registry.tsx`\n350: - [ ] For factory methods: `client.callSubid({ subid: \"factory.<method>\", input: {} })`\n351: - [ ] For schema hash integrity: verify blob hash before promoting gallery → catalog\n352: - [ ] Mark `wip: true` on nav entry until real backend is wired\n353: \n354: ---\n355: \n356: ## 11. Key File Locations\n357: \n358: | What | Path |\n359: |---|---|\n360: | Catalog contract (reference layout; inspect target first) | `/srv/git/3tched.com/src/json-render/catalog/catalog.ts` |\n361: | Component registry | `src/json-render/catalog/registry.tsx` |\n362: | Shell components | `src/json-render/catalog/components/shell.tsx` |\n363: | Dashboard widgets | `src/json-render/catalog/components/dashboard.tsx` |\n364: | Network components | `src/json-render/catalog/components/network.tsx` |\n365: | Primitives | `src/json-render/catalog/components/primitives.tsx` |\n366: | Nav manifest | `src/json-render/navigation/manifest.ts` |\n367: | Page specs index | `src/json-render/pages/index.ts` |\n368: | Shell spec | `src/json-render/shell/shellSpec.ts` |\n369: | Spec builders | `src/json-render/spec-builders.ts` |\n370: | Factory gRPC types | `src/grpc/gen/plugin_methods/factory__*.ts` |\n371: | gRPC client | `src/grpc/client.ts` |\n372: | json_render plugin | `/srv/git/odbus/crates/op-plugins/src/state_plugins/json_render.rs` |\n373: | schema_renderer plugin | `/srv/git/odbus/crates/op-plugins/src/state_plugins/schema_renderer.rs` |\n374: | tched_router plugin | `/srv/git/odbus/crates/op-plugins/src/state_plugins/tched_router.rs` |\n375: | Gallery/catalog routes | `/srv/git/odbus/crates/op-web/src/handlers/ui_model.rs` |\n376: | json-render library | `/srv/git/json-render/packages/core/src/` |\n377: | json-render.dev docs | https://json-render.dev/docs |"}
